src_raw_append\", \"src_async_contract\"], \"unknowns\": [], \"producers\": [\"B_ACK_QUEUE\"], \"consumers\": [\"B_ACK_CONSUME\"], \"invalidators\": [], \"reinterpreters\": [], \"unreferenced_behavior_ids\": [\"B_ACK_CONSUME\", \"B_ACK_QUEUE\"], \"coverage\": \"Described relationships; selected checks settle only their explicit scope\", \"candidate_ids\": []}, {\"fact_id\": \"F_MATCH\", \"source_ids\": [\"src_progress_update\", \"src_append_success\", \"src_reset\", \"src_tracker\", \"src_election\", \"src_changer\"], \"unknowns\": [], \"producers\": [\"B_ACK_CONSUME\", \"B_RESET\"], \"consumers\": [\"B_COMMIT\", \"B_CONFIG\", \"B_REPLICATION_SEND\"], \"invalidators\": [\"B_RESET\"], \"reinterpreters\": [\"B_CONFIG\"], \"unreferenced_behavior_ids\": [\"B_CONFIG\", \"B_REPLICATION_SEND\"], \"coverage\": \"Described relationships; selected checks settle only their explicit scope\", \"candidate_ids\": [\"57178805178846309132c23222858b0d\"]}, {\"fact_id\": \"F_LEADER_COMMIT\", \"source_ids\": [\"src_reset\", \"src_tracker\", \"src_log_commit\", \"src_raw_entry\", \"src_startup\", \"src_cfg_activation\"], \"unknowns\": [], \"producers\": [\"B_COMMIT\"], \"consumers\": [\"B_READY\", \"B_REPLICATION_SEND\"], \"invalidators\": [], \"reinterpreters\": [], \"unreferenced_behavior_ids\": [\"B_COMMIT\", \"B_READY\", \"B_REPLICATION_SEND\"], \"coverage\": \"Described relationships; selected checks settle only their explicit scope\", \"candidate_ids\": []}, {\"fact_id\": \"F_LEADER_ROLE\", \"source_ids\": [\"src_election\", \"src_transfer_roles\", \"src_tracker\", \"src_startup\"], \"unknowns\": [], \"producers\": [\"B_ELECT\"], \"consumers\": [\"B_RESET\"], \"invalidators\": [\"B_RESET\"], \"reinterpreters\": [], \"unreferenced_behavior_ids\": [\"B_ELECT\", \"B_RESET\"], \"coverage\": \"Described relationships; selected checks settle only their explicit scope\", \"candidate_ids\": []}, {\"fact_id\": \"F_ACCEPTED_HISTORY\", \"source_ids\": [\"src_append_handler\", \"src_log_append\", \"src_unstable_replace\", \"src_log_term_view\", \"src_log_slice_view\", \"src_unstable_view_contract\"], \"unknowns\": [], \"producers\": [\"B_ACCEPT_HISTORY\"], \"consumers\": [\"B_STORAGE_FRONTIER\", \"B_LOG_VIEW\"], \"invalidators\": [\"B_ACCEPT_HISTORY\", \"B_RECONSTRUCT\"], \"reinterpreters\": [], \"unreferenced_behavior_ids\": [\"B_RECONSTRUCT\"], \"coverage\": \"Described relationships; selected checks settle only their explicit scope\", \"candidate_ids\": [\"38f753379f86414bb955bff26a489d65\"]}], \"paused_candidate_ids\": [], \"comparison_refs\": [\"surface:Ready synchronous send ordering documentation\"], \"behavior_unknowns\": [{\"behavior_id\": \"B_ACK_QUEUE\", \"activity\": \"A6\", \"unknowns\": [\"The synchronous overlap wording in doc.go differs from the stricter Ready field contracts.\"], \"source_ids\": [\"src_send\", \"src_raw_entry\", \"src_raw_append\", \"src_async_contract\"], \"protections\": []}, {\"behavior_id\": \"B_READ_CONTEXT\", \"activity\": \"A2\", \"unknowns\": [\"Lease-based mode timing assumptions and full response construction are not fully traced; no read safety conclusion is made.\"], \"source_ids\": [\"src_pending_reads\", \"src_read_tracker\", \"src_replication_retry\", \"src_leader_proposal\", \"src_reset\", \"src_transfer_roles\", \"src_ready_contract\"], \"protections\": []}], \"surfaces\": [{\"entry_point\": \"raft.newRaft / raft.restore / confchange.Restore\", \"disposition\": \"mapped\", \"behavior_ids\": [\"B_RECONSTRUCT\", \"B_CONFIG\"], \"reason\": \"Startup and snapshot reconstruct log/configuration through read callees; deeper caller crash-atomic storage policies remain external.\", \"source_ids\": [\"src_startup\", \"src_snapshot_restore\", \"src_restore_config\", \"src_changer\"], \"high_consequence\": true}, {\"entry_point\": \"RawNode.Advance / raftLog.nextCommittedEnts / raftLog.acceptApplying\", \"disposition\": \"mapped\", \"behavior_ids\": [\"B_READY\", \"B_STORAGE_FRONTIER\"], \"reason\": \"Traced issuance, stability eligibility and completion effects; emitted work remains distinct from completed caller work.\", \"source_ids\": [\"src_log_delivery\", \"src_log_frontiers\", \"src_raw_advance\", \"src_applied\"], \"high_consequence\": true}, {\"entry_point\": \"raft.reset self Progress.Match initialization\", \"disposition\": \"mapped\", \"behavior_ids\": [\"B_RESET\", \"B_COMMIT\", \"B_ELECT\"], \"reason\": \"Self Match is initialized from local lastIndex, which can include unstable history; current-term commit check is the known protection to investigate before treating Match as durable support.\", \"source_ids\": [\"src_reset\", \"src_election\", \"src_log_commit\"], \"high_consequence\": true}, {\"entry_point\": \"Ready synchronous send ordering documentation\", \"disposition\": \"deferred\", \"behavior_ids\": [], \"reason\": \"doc.go describes same-batch overlap while Ready.Messages and Ready.Entries require persistence first. A harness can comply with the stricter common subset; permissive-order responsibility remains unresolved.\", \"source_ids\": [\"src_usage\", \"src_ready_contract\"], \"high_consequence\": true}], \"core_regions\": [{\"activity\": \"A1\", \"entries\": [{\"class_id\": \"A1\", \"applicability\": \"applicable\", \"purpose\": \"Qualified support for decision formation and legal advancement\", \"realization_summary\": \"Proposal append, deferred acknowledgment and voter quorum/current-term commit processing.\", \"entry_points\": [], \"behavior_ids\": [\"B_PROPOSE\", \"B_ACK_CONSUME\", \"B_COMMIT\", \"B_ACCEPT_HISTORY\"], \"variants\": [], \"unknowns\": [], \"source_ids\": [\"src_leader_proposal\", \"src_append_success\", \"src_log_commit\"]}], \"coverage\": \"Partial understanding; neither candidate labels nor object counts establish exhaustion\"}, {\"activity\": \"A2\", \"entries\": [{\"class_id\": \"A2\", \"applicability\": \"applicable\", \"purpose\": \"Context validity and acquisition, transfer or loss of authority\", \"realization_summary\": \"Election, term/role reset, transfer and quorum-loss handling.\", \"entry_points\": [], \"behavior_ids\": [\"B_ELECT\", \"B_RESET\", \"B_READ_CONTEXT\"], \"variants\": [], \"unknowns\": [], \"source_ids\": [\"src_election\", \"src_step\", \"src_transfer_roles\"]}], \"coverage\": \"Partial understanding; neither candidate labels nor object counts establish exhaustion\"}]}\nnext_objective {\"action\": \"investigate_and_refocus\", \"boundary\": \"both_core_paths\", \"reason\": \"Use results to update understanding and compare the remaining frontier\", \"core_gaps\": []}\n// Copyright 2015 The etcd Authors\n//\n// Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0 (the \"License\");\n// you may not use this file except in compliance with the License.\n// You may obtain a copy of the License at\n//\n//     http://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\n//\n// Unless required by applicable law or agreed to in writing, software\n// distributed under the License is distributed on an \"AS IS\" BASIS,\n// WITHOUT WARRANTIES OR CONDITIONS OF ANY KIND, either express or implied.\n// See the License for the specific language governing permissions and\n// limitations under the License.\n\npackage raft\n\nimport (\n\tpb \"go.etcd.io/raft/v3/raftpb\"\n)\n\nfunc applyToStore(_ []pb.Entry)      {}\nfunc sendMessages(_ []pb.Message)    {}\nfunc saveStateToDisk(_ pb.HardState) {}\nfunc saveToDisk(_ []pb.Entry)        {}\n\nfunc ExampleNode() {\n\tc := &Config{}\n\tn := StartNode(c, nil)\n\tdefer n.Stop()\n\n\t// stuff to n happens in other goroutines\n\n\t// the last known state\n\tvar prev pb.HardState\n\tfor {\n\t\t// Ready blocks until there is new state ready.\n\t\trd := <-n.Ready()\n\t\tif !isHardStateEqual(prev, rd.HardState) {\n\t\t\tsaveStateToDisk(rd.HardState)\n\t\t\tprev = rd.HardState\n\t\t}\n\n\t\tsaveToDisk(rd.Entries)\n\t\tgo applyToStore(rd.CommittedEntries)\n\t\tsendMessages(rd.Messages)\n\t}\n}\n../agent-source/README.md:12:To keep the codebase small as well as provide flexibility, the library only implements the Raft algorithm; both network and disk IO are left to the user. Library users must implement their own transportation layer for message passing between Raft peers over the wire. Similarly, users must implement their own storage layer to persist the Raft log and state.\n../agent-source/README.md:14:In order to easily test the Raft library, its behavior should be deterministic. To achieve this determinism, the library models Raft as a state machine.  The state machine takes a `Message` as input. A message can either be a local timer update or a network message sent from a remote peer. The state machine's output is a 3-tuple `{[]Messages, []LogEntries, NextState}` consisting of an array of `Messages`, `log entries`, and `Raft state changes`. For state machines with the same state, the same state machine input should always generate the same state machine output.\n../agent-source/README.md:93:  // Recover the in-memory storage from persistent snapshot, state and entries.\n../agent-source/README.md:114:First, read from the Node.Ready() channel and process the updates it contains. These steps may be performed in parallel, except as noted in step 2.\n../agent-source/README.md:116:1. Write Entries, HardState and Snapshot to persistent storage in order, i.e. Entries first, then HardState and Snapshot if they are not empty. If persistent storage supports atomic writes then all of them can be written together. Note that when writing an Entry with Index i, any previously-persisted entries with Index >= i must be discarded.\n../agent-source/README.md:118:2. Send all Messages to the nodes named in the To field. It is important that no messages be sent until the latest HardState has been persisted to disk, and all Entries written by any previous Ready batch (Messages may be sent while entries from the same batch are being persisted). To reduce the I/O latency, an optimization can be applied to make leader write to disk in parallel with its followers (as explained at section 10.2.1 in Raft thesis). If any Message has type MsgSnap, call Node.ReportSnapshot() after it has been sent (these messages may be large). Note: Marshalling messages is not thread-safe; it is important to make sure that no new entries are persisted while marshalling. The easiest way to achieve this is to serialise the messages directly inside the main raft loop.\n../agent-source/README.md:120:3. Apply Snapshot (if any) and CommittedEntries to the state machine. If any committed Entry has Type EntryConfChange, call Node.ApplyConfChange() to apply it to the node. The configuration change may be cancelled at this point by setting the NodeID field to zero before calling ApplyConfChange (but ApplyConfChange must be called one way or the other, and the decision to cancel must be based solely on the state machine and not external information such as the observed health of the node).\n../agent-source/README.md:122:4. Call Node.Advance() to signal readiness for the next batch of updates. This may be done at any time after step 1, although all updates must be processed in the order they were returned by Ready.\n../agent-source/README.md:124:Second, all persisted log entries must be made available via an implementation of the Storage interface. The provided MemoryStorage type can be used for this (if repopulating its state upon a restart), or a custom disk-backed implementation can be supplied.\n../agent-source/README.md:143:    case rd := <-s.Node.Ready():\n../agent-source/README.md:144:      saveToStorage(rd.HardState, rd.Entries, rd.Snapshot)\n../agent-source/README.md:145:      send(rd.Messages)\n../agent-source/README.md:149:      for _, entry := range rd.CommittedEntries {\n../agent-source/README.md:157:      s.Node.Advance()\n../agent-source/node.go:39:// The state is volatile and does not need to be persisted to the WAL.\n../agent-source/node.go:49:// Ready encapsulates the entries and messages that are ready to read,\n../agent-source/node.go:51:// All fields in Ready are read-only.\n../agent-source/node.go:52:type Ready struct {\n../agent-source/node.go:59:\t// Messages are sent.\n../agent-source/node.go:65:\t// Messages slice.\n../agent-source/node.go:74:\t// Entries specifies entries to be saved to stable storage BEFORE\n../agent-source/node.go:75:\t// Messages are sent.\n../agent-source/node.go:79:\t// Messages slice.\n../agent-source/node.go:80:\tEntries []pb.Entry\n../agent-source/node.go:86:\t// Messages slice.\n../agent-source/node.go:89:\t// CommittedEntries specifies entries to be committed to a\n../agent-source/node.go:95:\t// Messages slice.\n../agent-source/node.go:96:\tCommittedEntries []pb.Entry\n../agent-source/node.go:98:\t// Messages specifies outbound messages.\n../agent-source/node.go:101:\t// AFTER Entries are appended to stable storage.\n../agent-source/node.go:110:\tMessages []pb.Message\n../agent-source/node.go:112:\t// MustSync indicates whether the HardState and Entries must be durably\n../agent-source/node.go:158:\t// Ready returns a channel that returns the current point-in-time state.\n../agent-source/node.go:159:\t// Users of the Node must call Advance after retrieving the state returned by Ready (unless\n../agent-source/node.go:162:\t// NOTE: No committed entries from the next Ready may be applied until all committed entries\n../agent-source/node.go:164:\tReady() <-chan Ready\n../agent-source/node.go:166:\t// Advance notifies the Node that the application has saved progress up to the last Ready.\n../agent-source/node.go:167:\t// It prepares the node to return the next available Ready.\n../agent-source/node.go:169:\t// The application should generally call Advance after it applies the entries in last Ready.\n../agent-source/node.go:171:\t// However, as an optimization, the application may call Advance while it is applying the\n../agent-source/node.go:172:\t// commands. For example. when the last Ready contains a snapshot, the application might take\n../agent-source/node.go:173:\t// a long time to apply the snapshot data. To continue receiving Ready without blocking raft\n../agent-source/node.go:174:\t// progress, it can call Advance before finishing applying the last ready.\n../agent-source/node.go:176:\t// NOTE: Advance must not be called when using AsyncStorageWrites. Response messages from the\n../agent-source/node.go:178:\tAdvance()\n../agent-source/node.go:181:\t// change is observed in Ready.CommittedEntries, except when the app decides\n../agent-source/node.go:307:\treadyc     chan Ready\n../agent-source/node.go:323:\t\treadyc:     make(chan Ready),\n../agent-source/node.go:350:\tvar readyc chan Ready\n../agent-source/node.go:352:\tvar rd Ready\n../agent-source/node.go:359:\t\tif advancec == nil && n.rn.HasReady() {\n../agent-source/node.go:360:\t\t\t// Populate a Ready. Note that this Ready is not guaranteed to\n../agent-source/node.go:364:\t\t\t// the Ready again. We could instead force the previous Ready to be\n../agent-source/node.go:365:\t\t\t// handled first, but it's generally good to emit larger Readys plus\n../agent-source/node.go:441:\t\t\tn.rn.acceptReady(rd)\n../agent-source/node.go:445:\t\t\t\trd = Ready{}\n../agent-source/node.go:449:\t\t\tn.rn.Advance(rd)\n../agent-source/node.go:450:\t\t\trd = Ready{}\n../agent-source/node.go:475:\treturn n.stepWait(ctx, pb.Message{Type: pb.MsgProp, Entries: []pb.Entry{{Data: data}}})\n../agent-source/node.go:492:\treturn pb.Message{Type: pb.MsgProp, Entries: []pb.Entry{{Type: typ, Data: data}}}, nil\n../agent-source/node.go:552:func (n *node) Ready() <-chan Ready { return n.readyc }\n../agent-source/node.go:554:func (n *node) Advance() {\n../agent-source/node.go:614:\treturn n.step(ctx, pb.Message{Type: pb.MsgReadIndex, Entries: []pb.Entry{{Data: rctx}}})\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '108,165p' ../agent-source/README.md; sed -n '149,190p' ../agent-source/node.go; grep -R -n --include='*.go' 'rd.Messages\\\\|rd.Entries' ../agent-source/rafttest | head -45; cat ../agent-source/design.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '108,165p' ../agent-source/README.md; sed -n '149,190p' ../agent-source/node.go; grep -R -n --include='*.go' 'rd.Messages\\\\|rd.Entries' ../agent-source/rafttest | head -45; cat ../agent-source/design.md\"","aggregated_output":"  // Peer information is already included in the storage.\n  n := raft.RestartNode(c)\n```\n\nAfter creating a Node, the user has a few responsibilities:\n\nFirst, read from the Node.Ready() channel and process the updates it contains. These steps may be performed in parallel, except as noted in step 2.\n\n1. Write Entries, HardState and Snapshot to persistent storage in order, i.e. Entries first, then HardState and Snapshot if they are not empty. If persistent storage supports atomic writes then all of them can be written together. Note that when writing an Entry with Index i, any previously-persisted entries with Index >= i must be discarded.\n\n2. Send all Messages to the nodes named in the To field. It is important that no messages be sent until the latest HardState has been persisted to disk, and all Entries written by any previous Ready batch (Messages may be sent while entries from the same batch are being persisted). To reduce the I/O latency, an optimization can be applied to make leader write to disk in parallel with its followers (as explained at section 10.2.1 in Raft thesis). If any Message has type MsgSnap, call Node.ReportSnapshot() after it has been sent (these messages may be large). Note: Marshalling messages is not thread-safe; it is important to make sure that no new entries are persisted while marshalling. The easiest way to achieve this is to serialise the messages directly inside the main raft loop.\n\n3. Apply Snapshot (if any) and CommittedEntries to the state machine. If any committed Entry has Type EntryConfChange, call Node.ApplyConfChange() to apply it to the node. The configuration change may be cancelled at this point by setting the NodeID field to zero before calling ApplyConfChange (but ApplyConfChange must be called one way or the other, and the decision to cancel must be based solely on the state machine and not external information such as the observed health of the node).\n\n4. Call Node.Advance() to signal readiness for the next batch of updates. This may be done at any time after step 1, although all updates must be processed in the order they were returned by Ready.\n\nSecond, all persisted log entries must be made available via an implementation of the Storage interface. The provided MemoryStorage type can be used for this (if repopulating its state upon a restart), or a custom disk-backed implementation can be supplied.\n\nThird, after receiving a message from another node, pass it to Node.Step:\n\n```go\n\tfunc recvRaftRPC(ctx context.Context, m raftpb.Message) {\n\t\tn.Step(ctx, m)\n\t}\n```\n\nFinally, call `Node.Tick()` at regular intervals (probably via a `time.Ticker`). Raft has two important timeouts: heartbeat and the election timeout. However, internally to the raft package time is represented by an abstract \"tick\".\n\nThe total state machine handling loop will look something like this:\n\n```go\n  for {\n    select {\n    case <-s.Ticker:\n      n.Tick()\n    case rd := <-s.Node.Ready():\n      saveToStorage(rd.HardState, rd.Entries, rd.Snapshot)\n      send(rd.Messages)\n      if !raft.IsEmptySnap(rd.Snapshot) {\n        processSnapshot(rd.Snapshot)\n      }\n      for _, entry := range rd.CommittedEntries {\n        process(entry)\n        if entry.Type == raftpb.EntryConfChange {\n          var cc raftpb.ConfChange\n          cc.Unmarshal(entry.Data)\n          s.Node.ApplyConfChange(cc)\n        }\n      }\n      s.Node.Advance()\n    case <-s.done:\n      return\n    }\n  }\n```\n\nTo propose changes to the state machine from the node to take application data, serialize it into a byte slice and call:\n\n\t// consensus, notably including replacing a voter. Passing a ConfChangeV2\n\t// message is only allowed if all Nodes participating in the cluster run a\n\t// version of this library aware of the V2 API. See pb.ConfChangeV2 for\n\t// usage details and semantics.\n\tProposeConfChange(ctx context.Context, cc pb.ConfChangeI) error\n\n\t// Step advances the state machine using the given message. ctx.Err() will be returned, if any.\n\tStep(ctx context.Context, msg pb.Message) error\n\n\t// Ready returns a channel that returns the current point-in-time state.\n\t// Users of the Node must call Advance after retrieving the state returned by Ready (unless\n\t// async storage writes is enabled, in which case it should never be called).\n\t//\n\t// NOTE: No committed entries from the next Ready may be applied until all committed entries\n\t// and snapshots from the previous one have finished.\n\tReady() <-chan Ready\n\n\t// Advance notifies the Node that the application has saved progress up to the last Ready.\n\t// It prepares the node to return the next available Ready.\n\t//\n\t// The application should generally call Advance after it applies the entries in last Ready.\n\t//\n\t// However, as an optimization, the application may call Advance while it is applying the\n\t// commands. For example. when the last Ready contains a snapshot, the application might take\n\t// a long time to apply the snapshot data. To continue receiving Ready without blocking raft\n\t// progress, it can call Advance before finishing applying the last ready.\n\t//\n\t// NOTE: Advance must not be called when using AsyncStorageWrites. Response messages from the\n\t// local append and apply threads take its place.\n\tAdvance()\n\t// ApplyConfChange applies a config change (previously passed to\n\t// ProposeConfChange) to the node. This must be called whenever a config\n\t// change is observed in Ready.CommittedEntries, except when the app decides\n\t// to reject the configuration change (i.e. treats it as a noop instead), in\n\t// which case it must not be called.\n\t//\n\t// Returns an opaque non-nil ConfState protobuf which must be recorded in\n\t// snapshots.\n\tApplyConfChange(cc pb.ConfChangeI) *pb.ConfState\n\n\t// TransferLeadership attempts to transfer leadership to the given transferee.\n\tTransferLeadership(ctx context.Context, lead, transferee uint64)\n../agent-source/rafttest/interaction_env_handler_process_ready.go:52:\t\tif err := processAppend(n, rd.HardState, rd.Entries, rd.Snapshot); err != nil {\n../agent-source/rafttest/interaction_env_handler_process_ready.go:60:\tfor _, m := range rd.Messages {\n../agent-source/rafttest/node.go:81:\t\t\t\tn.storage.Append(rd.Entries)\n../agent-source/rafttest/node.go:85:\t\t\t\tfor _, m := range rd.Messages {\n## Progress\n\nProgress represents a follower’s progress in the view of the leader. Leader maintains progresses of all followers, and sends `replication message` to the follower based on its progress. \n\n`replication message` is a `msgApp` with log entries.\n\nA progress has two attribute: `match` and `next`. `match` is the index of the highest known matched entry. If leader knows nothing about follower’s replication status, `match` is set to zero. `next` is the index of the first entry that will be replicated to the follower. Leader puts entries from `next` to its latest one in next `replication message`.\n\nA progress is in one of the three state: `probe`, `replicate`, `snapshot`. \n\n```\n                            +--------------------------------------------------------+          \n                            |                  send snapshot                         |          \n                            |                                                        |          \n                  +---------+----------+                                  +----------v---------+\n              +--->       probe        |                                  |      snapshot      |\n              |   |  max inflight = 1  <----------------------------------+  max inflight = 0  |\n              |   +---------+----------+                                  +--------------------+\n              |             |            1. snapshot success                                    \n              |             |               (next=snapshot.index + 1)                           \n              |             |            2. snapshot failure                                    \n              |             |               (no change)                                         \n              |             |            3. receives msgAppResp(rej=false&&index>lastsnap.index)\n              |             |               (match=m.index,next=match+1)                        \nreceives msgAppResp(rej=true)                                                                   \n(next=match+1)|             |                                                                   \n              |             |                                                                   \n              |             |                                                                   \n              |             |   receives msgAppResp(rej=false&&index>match)                     \n              |             |   (match=m.index,next=match+1)                                    \n              |             |                                                                   \n              |             |                                                                   \n              |             |                                                                   \n              |   +---------v----------+                                                        \n              |   |     replicate      |                                                        \n              +---+  max inflight = n  |                                                        \n                  +--------------------+                                                        \n```\n\nWhen the progress of a follower is in `probe` state, leader sends at most one `replication message` per heartbeat interval. The leader sends `replication message` slowly and probing the actual progress of the follower. A `msgHeartbeatResp` or a `msgAppResp` with reject might trigger the sending of the next `replication message`.\n\nWhen the progress of a follower is in `replicate` state, leader sends `replication message`, then optimistically increases `next` to the latest entry sent. This is an optimized state for fast replicating log entries to the follower.\n\nWhen the progress of a follower is in `snapshot` state, leader stops sending any `replication message`.\n\nA newly elected leader sets the progresses of all the followers to `probe` state with `match` = 0 and `next` = last index. The leader slowly (at most once per heartbeat) sends `replication message` to the follower and probes its progress.\n\nA progress changes to `replicate` when the follower replies with a non-rejection `msgAppResp`, which implies that it has matched the index sent. At this point, leader starts to stream log entries to the follower fast. The progress will fall back to `probe` when the follower replies a rejection `msgAppResp` or the link layer reports the follower is unreachable. We aggressively reset `next` to `match`+1 since if we receive any `msgAppResp` soon, both `match` and `next` will increase directly to the `index` in `msgAppResp`. (We might end up with sending some duplicate entries when aggressively reset `next` too low.  see open question)\n\nA progress changes from `probe` to `snapshot` when the follower falls very far behind and requires a snapshot. After sending `msgSnap`, the leader waits until the success, failure or abortion of the previous snapshot sent. The progress will go back to `probe` after the sending result is applied.\n\n### Flow Control\n\n1. limit the max size of message sent per message. Max should be configurable.\nLower the cost at probing state as we limit the size per message; lower the penalty when aggressively decreased to a too low `next`\n\n2. limit the # of in flight messages < N when in `replicate` state. N should be configurable. Most implementation will have a sending buffer on top of its actual network transport layer (not blocking raft node). We want to make sure raft does not overflow that buffer, which can cause message dropping and triggering a bunch of unnecessary resending repeatedly. \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
